7ba29480-685c-4292-8e59-547fa2b273ca	96ef61d6-c3a1-4db9-b0d4-6f91b966f38c	joe h	\N	5.00	\N	This is a beautiful church. The architecture is incredible as is the history inside it. There is a lot to see and the self-guided tour offers a really nice explanation of the history. If you’re a history buff, check out the exterior east wall and you will see damage from WW2 that is still visible. Also cool is the oldest door in the UK (almost 1,000 years old), which you can find as you walk towards the Chapter House chamber.	3 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2tVME9IaHlhMVpDY0ZoRlFWZDNiVXQzWVZsZlpFRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0xda8effa2059b537a!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOkU0OHhya1ZCcFhFQVd3bUt3YVlfZEE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-10 12:31:40.973568+05:30
a7c24a06-caee-49a3-8415-f11ae022c16c	96ef61d6-c3a1-4db9-b0d4-6f91b966f38c	Haydee b. Lopez	\N	5.00	\N	Westminster Abbey is absolutely breathtaking and deeply moving. From the moment you step inside, you feel the weight of history, faith, and artistry all in one place. The architecture is stunning—soaring ceilings, intricate details, and a sense of reverence that fills every corner.<br><br>Walking through the Abbey feels like walking through centuries of stories. The statues, memorials, and tombs are powerful and humbling, and the stained-glass windows are simply beautiful, especially when the light shines through them. Every detail invites you to pause, reflect, and admire the craftsmanship.<br><br>It’s not just a historical landmark, but a spiritual and cultural experience. Whether you love history, architecture, or meaningful places, Westminster Abbey is a must-visit when you’re in London. Truly unforgettable and 100% worth it. 🤍✨	2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2pOeGJFRlNWMVJxWVhOeFkwMXNObk5TVFZSbFpVRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0xda8effa2059b537a!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOjNxbEFSV1RqYXNxY01sNnNSTVRlZUE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-10 12:31:40.973568+05:30
0b042d15-1967-4ba9-8f78-3be59e69542f	96ef61d6-c3a1-4db9-b0d4-6f91b966f38c	Shanz Shanz	\N	5.00	\N	My Visit to Westminster and Westminster Abbey, London<br><br>My visit to Westminster and Westminster Abbey was one of the most memorable parts of my London trip. Located in the heart of the city near the Houses of Parliament and Big Ben, this area is filled with history, architecture, and national heritage. Walking through Westminster felt like stepping into Britain’s political and royal past.<br><br>Westminster Abbey, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is an architectural masterpiece dating back to the 11th century. It has hosted countless royal coronations, weddings, and funerals, including those of monarchs and national heroes. Inside, I was amazed by the stained-glass windows, Gothic ceilings, royal tombs, and the Poets’ Corner, where great writers like Shakespeare and Dickens are commemorated.<br><br>Visitor Tips: Tickets can be booked online for faster entry. Photography is not allowed inside the Abbey, so take time to appreciate its quiet beauty. Attend a choral service or Evensong if you can — it’s a truly moving experience. The area around Westminster is best explored on foot, with easy access to Big Ben, the Palace of Westminster, and the River Thames.<br><br>How to Reach: The Abbey is located at 20 Deans Yard, London SW1P 3PA. The nearest Underground station is Westminster (Jubilee, District, and Circle lines), just a short walk away.<br><br>Overall, Westminster and Westminster Abbey offer a deep connection to British history and culture — a must-visit for anyone exploring London’s most iconic landmarks.	5 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2tGWVNtNTBSR0k1VURaUVR5MTNkRkJHY0d0eGVYYxAB!2m1!1s0x0:0xda8effa2059b537a!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOkFYSm50RGI5UDZQTy13dFBGcGtxeXc%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-10 12:31:40.973568+05:30
d53bfb17-ab0b-40d2-a3a8-17e98e960cdf	96ef61d6-c3a1-4db9-b0d4-6f91b966f38c	Andrew Larkin	\N	5.00	\N	So much history here.  Well worth the extra £5 to go up to the galleries.  Hundreds of famous people are buried here, with a whole rangs of different monuments.  There is also the oldest image of an angel in Europe (although a bit difficult to make out now).  It is really worth taking the time to speak to the guides, they are so knowledgeable and happy to speak to you.	Edited 2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2xoWlpWTktXRTkxZVVoS2RXSkdSbTVrT0ZvMWJsRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0xda8effa2059b537a!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOlhZZVNKWE91eUhKdWJGRm5kOFo1blE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-10 12:31:40.973568+05:30
bdc1ee51-f172-4821-9953-e35767bef96e	96ef61d6-c3a1-4db9-b0d4-6f91b966f38c	Pierre mAScriER	\N	5.00	\N	5 stars is the minimum you can give for such a building. Gothic, impressive, with so many things to see that 1 tour is not sufficient to cover all there is to see and discover.<br>The organisation reflect the success of the visits, 7 days a week. The signage is perfect, the loos are well indicated as well as the restaurant/café, the shop and the many different areas of the cathedral.<br>And finally the staff: helpful, polite and constantly smiling while we take pictures. They have a decent knowledge about the he Abbey and are keen to share it.<br>I couldn't fault anything and found nothing negative to say, which is not often...	Edited 2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT25aTGJsSlJaMnhrWkd0d1lXUm9TV1Z2UzB0ME1WRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0xda8effa2059b537a!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOnZLblJRZ2xkZGtwYWRoSWVvS0t0MVE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-10 12:31:40.973568+05:30
7dd69f32-34a9-411e-b3e9-e90abc6108bb	96ef61d6-c3a1-4db9-b0d4-6f91b966f38c	Mioma Priora	\N	5.00	\N	Standing before Westminster Abbey is to stand at the very heart of a nation's narrative. My visit was less a simple tour and more an overwhelming immersion into a thousand years of ceremony, memory, and stone.<br>The first impact is architectural: the soaring Gothic vaults and flying buttresses are a breathtaking testament to medieval faith and ambition. Yet, the true weight of the place is historical. You are walking on a floor of poets, philosophers, and monarchs. The experience of standing before the Tomb of the Unknown Warrior or the simple stone marking Charles Darwin is profoundly moving—a collision of the sacred, the scientific, and the state.<br>However, this is no silent mausoleum. It is a living, working church and one of London's most visited sites. The crowds are inescapable, and the quiet reverence you seek is often punctuated by the shuffle of feet and multilingual guides. To find its soul, I recommend attending a service or the sublime choral evensong, where the building's purpose is fully realized in music and liturgy.<br>The Abbey doesn't just display history; it embodies it. You leave impressed, certainly, but also thoughtfully grappling with the complex tapestry of power, art, and belief it represents. It is an essential, magnificent, and uniquely demanding experience.	2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2xBeGExWXlOVUV6Ukd4TWRYVk1hRVZWYVZONGRVRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0xda8effa2059b537a!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOlAxa1YyNUEzRGxMdXVMaEVVaVN4dUE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-10 12:31:40.973568+05:30
