014b8a55-8c8c-4b00-a882-ca4bc1dcdbdf	374bf84d-1223-40e7-b0a1-c56966ae9eee	Joanna S	\N	5.00	\N	Great experience. One of the best things to do in London when you visiting. You can see many monuments from a different perspective than is available from land. Plus, there's a guide on the ship who shares stories and interesting facts about the buildings we see. Our guide was Mitch, a great guy. I recommend buying a ticket for the last sunset cruise, with fantastic views.	7 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2s5WGVqTjFhRVJLU0d4dFpVdFNTVTl4WlZkdk5rRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x61a09eaf07649a32!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOk9XejN1aERKSGxtZUtSSU9xZVdvNkE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 14:25:16.485964+05:30
a8310b71-84db-4599-b208-9a15fca8c6cd	374bf84d-1223-40e7-b0a1-c56966ae9eee	wMas	\N	5.00	\N	The London Eye and the River’s Reflection<br><br>Long ago, the River Thames flowed quietly through the heart of London, watching the city grow and change. For hundreds of years, it had seen kings and queens, ships full of treasures, and bridges rise and fall.<br><br>Then, at the turn of the new millennium, the river saw something new being built on its southern bank — something tall, bright, and round. It was the London Eye, a giant wheel made of glass and steel. The people called it the Millennium Wheel, and it was meant to celebrate the year 2000 — a symbol of looking forward to the future.	5 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2paa2JFeDNOV3RCZFZWTlgzTktZMlJGWVZONVEzYxAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x61a09eaf07649a32!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOjZkbEx3NWtBdVVNX3NKY2RFYVN5Q3c%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 14:25:16.485964+05:30
5d8dc7f9-f58a-4da9-aa05-f982bf34be24	374bf84d-1223-40e7-b0a1-c56966ae9eee	Antonio Cordeiro Roza	\N	5.00	\N	A wonderful experience with animals – perfect for families and amazing views!<br><br>We recently visited and were amazed by the number and variety of animals. All of them were very well cared for, showing the love and dedication of the staff. It’s a safe and pleasant environment, perfect for children – they enjoyed every minute!<br><br>The place also offers stunning views of the city. You can see all of London, including many of its most famous tourist spots. It’s a fantastic location for taking lots of beautiful photos in different areas – perfect for a family day out.<br><br>With well-organized spaces, a peaceful atmosphere, and fair pricing, it’s an excellent choice for anyone looking to enjoy nature and take in the best views of the city. Highly recommended!	Edited 8 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2xGMk9IWndZVlJtTWs1dE56VlNjVmxMVVRaa2FrRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x61a09eaf07649a32!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOlF2OHZwYVRmMk5tNzVScVlLUTZkakE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 14:25:16.485964+05:30
c3a83241-6ead-45f6-8c50-b3adb8ce732f	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Kevin Costello	\N	5.00	\N	This place was an unexpected delight. So much to see. And if course the views once the sky had cleared all the way to the pyramids. The fortifications, the mosque and court...it's all spectacular. And the view to the sandstone hills beyond are great. Then of course there's the police museum and and military museum included...leave half a day or more to truely do this complex justice!!!	a month ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2t4WmJFSnNNMWc1TUhaT1RXVnFiSEJtYVZWRlkzYxAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOkxZbEJsM1g5MHZOTWVqbHBmaVVFY3c%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
c46a1bb1-ae95-4a2d-a1d7-7f40958cf96f	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Dr KR Viswanthan	\N	4.00	\N	STANDING BEFORE CENTURIES: THE MAJESTIC POWER OF FORTRESS THAT COMMANDS RESPECT<br><br>Even from the outside, this historic fortress leaves a deep and lasting impression. Approaching the massive stone walls, one immediately senses the scale, strength, and authority the structure must have projected for centuries. The height of the fortifications, the thickness of the walls, and the commanding hilltop position create an atmosphere of awe and historical reverence.<br><br>Although we could not go inside, simply viewing the monument from the exterior was a memorable experience. The grand domes and slender minarets rising above the fortified walls create a striking visual contrast between military strength and architectural elegance. The structure seems to narrate stories of rulers, battles, and empires that shaped the region’s history.<br><br>The surroundings add to the experience — the open space outside allows visitors to fully appreciate the size and design of the complex. Watching the sunlight fall on the ancient stone gives a sense of timelessness.<br><br>This is one of those rare places where even a brief external visit feels meaningful. It inspires curiosity, respect for history, and admiration for the engineering and vision of past civilizations. Definitely a landmark worth seeing, even if only from the outside.	2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2xCc2VtaDBiVmt5V2prMGVXWlhTMWxGUVRscGFGRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOlBsemh0bVkyWjk0eWZXS1lFQTlpaFE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
b1bd588d-f291-410f-b037-a175da9da5e2	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Sky T	\N	2.00	\N	Its an absolute chaos to enter, doesn't matter how proud egyptians are of the citadel. Five electric gates, but only one for foreigners, and they don't tell you that while you are being shoved around in the crowd. Absolutely no crowd control, no line system. You will get to the gate, try to scan your ticket, i will not work. Someone out of at least 10 staff that look visibility bored will come up eventually and point out that you are a foreigner, yet this is the gate for Egyptians only. And when you will ask if the next gate allows foreigners he will walk away without answering.<br>No directions are posted inside. You will have to figure out where what is. The mosque is a one way syst, but you will find out only after walking around, "exit only" someone will say. Because putting a sign out would make it too convenient for people visiting. Someone at the toilets will ask for money for toilet paper, have a tissue in you handbag and ignore them.<br>Overall not sure if it's worth visiting. The mosque interior is pretty, but nothing impressive.<br>If you do go, get an online ticket, because the ticket office seemed down right agresive	2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2pCdWFWTmtlVGRMTmxsVVQzQkdhMVJzVFRVemJIYxAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOjBuaVNkeTdLNllUT3BGa1RsTTUzbHc%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
0df8d5bb-aca0-475b-9e17-f844f578bb24	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Fatih Cihan Çelebi	\N	5.00	\N	The mosque was spectacular. We were really blessed to see that. Also the fortress looks good. We really didn't care about the military museum but it was in the palaces so we were delighted to see them too.	2 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT25WRFgwMXBabVZWZWtoQ2FXNVRjRXA1WjJJeVpFRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOnVDX01pZmVVekhCaW5TcEp5Z2IyZEE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
cc509e49-59e7-4996-8536-9970e2873fd5	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Zubair	\N	5.00	\N	The Saladin Citadel was an truly astonishing monument and easily one of the highlights of our trip to Cairo. The engineering brilliance on display is staggering, particularly the Mosque of Muhammad Ali inside. Even having seen the Taj Mahal, I found this mosque to be one of the most exquisite marvels of history I have ever witnessed. While it is a bit of a blow to the site's "authenticity" to know that many of the stones were repurposed (or as we heard, "stolen") from the Giza pyramids, one must remember that this was the norm in those days—where the rule of law was simply the rule of the powerful.<br><br>The visitor experience was a mix of modern convenience and old-world "hustle." Despite the crowds at the entrance, ticketing was relatively easy. It is important for future visitors to know that paying with a credit card is much easier than tendering cash, as the site has moved toward a cashless system.<br>However, be wary once you are inside. The government-approved electric golf carts are run by men who, in my experience, will try to scam you. They pressure you to pay an upfront "tip"; I strongly advise against this. If you pay them at the start, they often stop showing up for you. You have to "keep it interesting" and make them feel that a reward is coming at the end to ensure they actually provide the service.<br><br>The National Military Museum was also fascinating, specifically the collection of ancient USSR weaponry and aircraft. However, the presentation felt a bit like a "propaganda machine"—the subtle (and not-so-subtle) nationalistic messaging was a bit of an overkill. Even here, the issue of solicitation persists; a cop at the entrance blatantly asked me for a "tip." I had to remind him that in most countries, giving a "tip" to a police officer is regarded as a bribe, which finally made him walk away.	3 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT25CSFNGQXlaRVpqVjNFemRWWnFlRFZLYTFadFpuYxAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOnBHSFAyZEZjV3EzdVZqeDVKa1ZtZnc%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
f97c6fdc-4916-4270-99c9-bbad0c1a3c5e	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Mark Sulkin	\N	3.00	\N	Great views of the city. If you've been on a tour, it's "been that done that" The Cairo Citadel was built on the orders of Saladin between 1176 and 1183 to protect the city from European attacks. Today, it's one of Cairo's main tourist attractions and offers privileged views of the city.	a month ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT25CWGVISkZTMnRsTm1JeVEzTmFVa0Z4UW1aMVozYxAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOnBXeHJFS2tlNmIyQ3NaUkFxQmZ1Z3c%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
e910fd05-8b6e-4b26-8cef-28b2f280ad76	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Dr. Arindam Ray	\N	5.00	\N	We visited the Citadel of Saladin on 19th October 2025, reaching around 2:00 PM during our Egypt trip from India. The entry ticket cost EGP 550 per person, and we chose a buddy ride (battery-operated car) for EGP 500 (for 3 persons) — highly recommended, as the fort complex is vast and built on a hilltop.<br><br>The Citadel is a magnificent historic fortress offering a panoramic view of Cairo. Inside, we explored impressive military exhibits, including fighter planes, tanks, and missiles, which reflect Egypt’s strong military heritage.<br><br>We also had a delightful interaction with a group of Egyptian schoolboys — cheerful, friendly, and curious — which added a warm local touch to our experience.<br><br>A highlight of our visit was the stunning mosque inside the Citadel. Entry required covering our shoes (EGP 5 each), and the interior was truly breathtaking — peaceful, beautifully designed, and full of spiritual charm.<br><br>✅ Ambiance: ★★★★★<br>✅ Administration: ★★★★★<br>✅ Staff Behaviour: ★★★★★<br><br>Clean surroundings, well-maintained pathways, and courteous staff made the visit memorable. We spent about 1.5 hours, but history lovers could easily spend more time exploring.<br><br>💡 Tips for Visitors:<br><br>Carry a cap or hat — it gets sunny on the hill.<br>Opt for the buddy ride if visiting with family or kids.<br>Cards are widely accepted at the ticket counter.<br><br>A must-visit landmark in Cairo — combining history, architecture, and heritage in one majestic place. Highly recommended!	5 months ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT25SWmVIZGhUVkZZVWkxb1dFMTZjV294UlhSUFpFRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOnRZeHdhTVFYUi1oWE16cWoxRXRPZEE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
1c3a44af-67a1-4298-a849-d80d65978057	c2929644-6b9f-4ada-b2af-b9e0f727ef2c	Aury	\N	5.00	\N	Last November 1st,2025, my friends and I returned to El Cairo from Aswan. We continued our tour visiting Old Cairo with Farouk who is a knowledgeable guide. He answered all of our questions. The Citadel of Cairo, is a medieval Islamic-era, built by Salah ad-Din (Saladin) in 1176. It's situated on the Mokattam hills, with spectacular city view.<br>The Citadel served as one of the oldest military fortresses built in the Middle Ages. Also, served as the seat of government and residence of rulers for nearly 700 years. The Citadel features stunning mosques like the Muhammad Ali Mosque and Al-Nasir Muhammad Mosque.<br>The fort provided protection against  any attack.<br>It's worth the visit.	a month ago	google	https://www.google.com/maps/reviews/data=!4m8!14m7!1m6!2m5!1sCi9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2xWWVMxRnRNbGRmUm1kcWEwOWljSEk1TFhOWU9FRRAB!2m1!1s0x0:0x2fdb96e4e9f72e6f!3m1!1s2@1:CAIQACodChtycF9oOlVYS1FtMldfRmdqa09icHI5LXNYOEE%7C%7C?hl=en-US	f	en	2026-04-16 16:49:00.136577+05:30
